<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pirot</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pirot is a town in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on the Nišava river, near the Bulgarian border, on the old road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Ottoman for over four centuries, it was taken by Serbia in the war of 1877 to 1878 and confirmed to it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a>. It is known for its woven kilim carpets.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pirot.jpg" alt="Pirot" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IvanaMadzarevic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pirot_fortress,_Serbia_11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
